  "response": "**Decision: request changes, don't block.**\n\nThe finding reports `byte size: 260,000 bytes (253.9 KB)` for `src/assets/hero-banner.png`. That lands at `low` severity — it crosses the 200 KB floor but is well below the 500 KB (medium) and 1000 KB (high) marks. A 254 KB hero on the landing page is heavier than it should be (it's the LCP candidate on every cold visit), but it's not a \"block the merge\" emergency.\n\n**Recommended transformation:**\n\n- **Resize to the actual render area.** A hero rendered at ~1280–1600 CSS px wide on desktop only needs a 2× source around `1600w × ~900h` (16:9) or `1600w × 720h` (~2.2:1 cinematic), not whatever full-bleed export the design tool spat out. Ship a `srcset` with `1600w / 1200w / 800w` candidates so phones aren't downloading the desktop master.\n- **Re-encode to AVIF, with a WebP fallback.** Hero LCP is exactly where AVIF's extra ~20–30% savings over WebP pay for themselves — every visitor pays this cost on first paint. AVIF support is universal across evergreen browsers now, but a `<picture>` with `image/avif` → `image/webp` → original PNG sources costs you nothing and protects ancient clients. Target ~60–80 KB AVIF at q≈50 for a 1600w hero; the PNG should disappear from the build entirely.\n\nIf the image is mostly flat colour, a UI gradient, or a stylised illustration, mention the `shrink_raster` action's parenthetical: an SVG or CSS gradient may replace it outright and zero out the asset weight.",
